\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1  - maximum rotation \line  - use `rotateRightMostDigits`\line  - 105 -->  051 -->  015 (then drop leading zero) \line  - 735291 --> 352917 --> 329175 --> 321759 --> 321597 --> 321579\line  - maximal rotation is when you've rotated the final two digits. You can't do any more, because you would have one too many digits fixed in place, leaving only the last digit left to 'rotate', which you can't do. \line\line\line  - how do we use the `rotateRightMostDigits` function?  - ah, we use the `count` in ascending order, using a smaller and smaller count with each iteration\line  - how do we know when to stop? when you rotate the final two digits, or when `count` is 2. \line - `count` starts at length of `argNum`\line\line P: \line Create a function that takes a number and returns a number representing its maximum rotation. The maximum rotation is a process that starts with the first digit being rotated to the left. On the subsequent iteration, the first digit is kept in place, and  second digit is rotated, and in the next iteration  the first two digits are kept in place and the remaining rotated, in the iteration after that the first three digits are kept in place and the remaining rotated. This process continues until we have rotated the last two digits and there is now only one digit left (and that can't be rotated)  \line\line input: number\line output: number \line\line rules - leading zeros to be removed\line if the count is two or less, end iteration \line\line E:\line see above\line\line D: \line strings\line\line A: \line  - initialise `strNum` to `argNum`, converted into a string \line - initialise `count` to the length of `strNum`\line - WHILE `count` is >= 2\line  -- reassign `strNum` to passing it in to `rotateRightMostDigits`, with `count` as the second argument\line -- decrement `count`\line - initialise `resNum` to : converting `strNum` to a number\line  - return `resNum`\line\par
